WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:01.650
Welcome and Opening Comments
Professor Sir Malcolm Grant, CBE

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.650
Chair, NHS England Board
Preamble

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:07.880
Good morning, everybody and welcome to this
meeting of the Board of NHS England.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.339
We are delighted this morning to welcome members
of the public who have joined us here in the

00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:19.779
meeting room in London and also those who
may be watching this session on the live web

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:20.839
stream.

00:00:20.839 --> 00:00:25.110
That is part of our commitment to openness
and transparency in all that we do.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:33.220
We are here for NHS England as an organisation
that belongs to the people and it is right

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:39.270
that there should be a spotlight cast on the
way in which decisions are taken by this organisation.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:44.200
Transparency
We meet in public; that means that meetings

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.710
are observed by members of the public.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:52.050
It is not an interactive meeting, I am afraid,
but we will be taking some very significant

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:58.920
items of business this morning and it is right
that they should be publically observed.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.180
We do not do all of our business in public:
there are times when we work together as a

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.900
team of Executive Directors and Non Executive
Directors on what we call 'development sessions',

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.640
as we are bringing new policies and new strategies
through to the point where we can bring them

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:19.020
to the public session; but inevitably, in
some of the complex areas that we are working

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.910
in, we need a great deal of build up time
and development of a mutual understanding

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:28.560
around the table before we can bring it to
public session.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.880
Secondly and immediately after this meeting,
we will go into private session, because there

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:42.120
are one or two items of business which are
not yet ready for public discussion.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.950
It is the sort of business which relates to
particular HR issues affecting individuals

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:52.760
or to serious financial decisions that we
need to work our way through before we publish

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.760
the outcomes.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.080
Annual General Meeting
Before I kick off this morning, I would like

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.920
to say a few words about what we did last
night.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.420
We hosted our first even Annual General Meeting.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.869
This is the first time -- I am advised -- that
the NHS nationally has ever done something

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:11.869
like this.

00:02:11.869 --> 00:02:17.920
It has not been uncommon for Trusts and Hospital
Trusts, etc., to have meetings such as this;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:25.019
but for us it was, I think, as exceptionally
interesting experience, not just because of

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:30.730
the formal material at the meeting -- which
was our reports on our first year in operation

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:36.540
prior to our actually becoming NHS England
-- but because it was preceded by a series

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.540
of workshops.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:43.829
There were five workshops, which were very
valuably facilitated by Olivia Butterworth

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:50.439
and her team, which examined various themes,
such as end of life care, the call to action

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:54.510
and other themes that are vital to our future.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:00.340
These themes were explored through the eyes
of patients, members of the public, interest

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.079
groups and other stakeholders, resulting in
a series of presentations to us which gave

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:09.040
us, I think, quite new and valuable insights.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.379
I have to say, for me, they persuaded me of
the extraordinary value of ensuring that everything

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:21.400
that we do is a matter of coproduction with
patients and public, who we are here to serve.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.269
As such, I would like publically to express
my thanks to all those who participated in

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:34.760
yesterday's session for making this AGM a
pioneering exercise in openness, but also

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:41.209
a learning exercise for us, as much as a reporting
and accountability exercise for us.

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:47.659
Apologies and declarations of interest
There are no apologies for absence from this

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:54.739
morning's Board meeting and I propose now
to move into the substantive business of the

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:56.430
Board.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.049
I want to take, first of all, any declarations
of interest.

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:06.139
I have not received any in advance, so I will
assume that there are none to declare other

00:04:06.139 --> 00:04:08.239
than those that are on our register.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:11.819
Appointment of Ed Smith as Deputy Chair
Secondly, as one preliminary item of business,

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:18.720
I should like to report -- I know with the
full support of the Board -- my proposal for

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.919
the appointment of Ed Smith, one of our Non
Executive Directors, to be Deputy Chair and

00:04:24.919 --> 00:04:29.030
Senior Independent Director of the Board of
NHS England.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.960
I am immensely grateful to Ed for agreeing
to take this on and indeed, it is no more

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.659
than a formalisation of the role which he
has already been fulfilling; and I am deeply

00:04:38.659 --> 00:04:45.860
conscious of my debt to him for the support
and assurance that he gives me.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:50.040
May I assume that that has unanimous consent
around the table?

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.580
Thank you very much.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:54.610
Safety
There is no fire alarm test due during this

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:00.009
meeting and if the alarm does sound, then
our staff will escort people from the building.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:03.020
Minutes of the last meeting
Let me, then, go straight into the substantive

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.610
business of the day.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.560
The first item is the minutes of the previous
meeting of 18th of July.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.699
I have not had any comments from Members of
the Board, so may I take them as an accurate

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:16.310
record of those proceedings?

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.270
Thank you very much.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:25.010
We have noted an action list regarding the
status of each of the actions from the previous

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:30.370
meetings of the Board, but I do not think
we need to dwell on that; it is a matter for

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.410
us to continue to chase progress.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:37.720
I might just invite, however, Tim Kelsey to
say something, briefly, on the Integrated

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.970
Performance Report and the couple of items
there.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:44.490
Tim, is there anything you would like to say
to us on that?

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:50.740
Tim Kelsey: We will be talking about the Report
later on.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.740
Malcolm Grant: Yes.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.169
Maybe you could wrap it up when we turn to
the Performance Report.

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:54.169
Tim Kelsey: Yes.

00:05:54.169 --> 00:06:03.379
Malcolm Grant: There were just a couple of
items and issues that we wanted to take on

00:06:03.379 --> 00:06:04.379
there.

00:06:04.379 --> 00:06:05.379
Where is Paul?

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:06.379
He is there.

00:06:06.379 --> 00:06:07.379
Paul Baumann: I am hiding in the corner.

00:06:07.379 --> 00:06:08.379
Malcolm Grant: Yes, right.

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:09.379
Is there anything you wanted to say on the
item regarding transparency in DH?

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:10.379
Paul Baumann: Yes.

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:11.379
The letter has been drafted, it is on its
way; we would expect a response shortly.

00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:14.460
That is all in hand.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.599
Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:23.910
Bruce, there was something on Academic Health
Science Networks; is that all in hand?

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:24.910
Bruce Keogh: Yes.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:25.910
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:30.550
We will come back to those issues, in part
at least, during the rest of the meeting this

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:31.550
morning.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.610
Let me, then, move on, if I may, to item three,
which is the Chief Executive's report.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:40.340
We have a written paper, but I am going to
invite David Nicholson to talk to us.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.340
David?

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.340
Chief Executive's Report
Sir David Nicholson

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.340
Chief Executive
Strategy

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.340
Overview
Thank you, Chairman.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.340
I assume people have read the report.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:46.340
There are two or three things that I want
to highlight on it.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:49.750
The first one is the work that we are doing
on strategy; and just to reinforce the importance

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:55.889
of this, when you think about the nature and
scale of the challenge of the NHS going forward,

00:06:55.889 --> 00:07:02.539
we really do need a strategic response and
I guess, a response that will go beyond the

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:03.539
electoral cycle.

00:07:03.539 --> 00:07:08.580
It seems to me that was one of the reasons
why NHS England was set up in the first place:

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.919
to be able to give us a medium- to longer-term
view about how we develop the NHS and in particular,

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:21.800
how we respond to the challenges that are
set out in the publication Call to Action,

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.449
which we have discussed at the Board a number
of times before.

00:07:25.449 --> 00:07:30.150
It sets out the nature and scale of the challenge,
it sets out the kind of direction that the

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:38.180
NHS might want to go in; but in a sense, heralds
a broader discussion about what is required

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.760
for the NHS going forward.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:45.620
There are a series of products that will come
out of that process, which we have discussed.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.689
Actions
There are a couple of things which have happened

00:07:48.689 --> 00:07:50.240
in relation to that.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:58.550
One of the products is the Urgent and Emergency
Care Review -- which is coming to fruition

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:03.449
and will be coming to the Board shortly -- which
will set out a vision and a direction for

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:09.159
urgent care and emergency care services across
the country, which I think will significantly

00:08:09.159 --> 00:08:13.539
impact on the way in which we think about
and deliver emergency services.

00:08:13.539 --> 00:08:20.919
Also, in August we published the report on
the first attempt to think about what the

00:08:20.919 --> 00:08:23.319
primary care strategy might look like.

00:08:23.319 --> 00:08:28.180
These are two, I think, important interventions
in what is developing as a significant set

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.520
of discussions.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.380
We have a communications plan in place.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:37.230
The Annual General Meeting was part of the
discussion that we are having with patients

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:42.200
and the public about the future of the NHS.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:50.780
There are a whole series of events, both locally,
run by CCGs and nationally, run by NHS England,

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:56.200
which will engage people in discussions about
how we can take the NHS forward.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:03.760
Underpinning all of that is the planning approach
-- which, again, we will be bringing to Board

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:11.130
-- which will require all Clinical Commissioning
Groups and indeed, all commissioners -- whether

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:15.390
they are Clinical Commissioning Groups or
ourselves -- to deliver five year forward

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:19.310
looks and two year financial operational plans
to underpin them.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.930
As such, there is really important, crunchy
work going on at the moment which I think

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:26.140
will make a big difference to the NHS going
forward.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:31.350
Mandate
As to the mandate, a critical part of the

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:40.850
way in which this organisation relates to
the Government, the consultation on that continues;

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.690
it ends at the end of September.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:50.030
We are thinking very carefully about our response
and how we are working around all that, but

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:53.600
there are two or three things I would say
about it.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:58.980
First of all, it is really important that
the relationship between ourselves and Government

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.800
is determined by improving outcomes.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:07.230
That seems, to me, critically important for
our patients and our public and really significant

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.230
for us.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:16.230
We have to make sure, for an NHS which increasingly
needs to be locally driven, that people have

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:23.500
the space locally to innovate and to take
services forward; we do not need an environment

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:26.980
which is cluttered for them with things.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:32.880
We also need to make sure that our relationship
with Government and the Department continues

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.900
to develop and improve.

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:41.430
We have more work to do to get everything
into place, but I am confident we will come

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.470
up with a good conclusion with the Department
at the end of this month.

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:49.420
We will set out, I think, clearly, what is
required through the mandate.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:56.801
Friends and Family Test
The third thing I want to mention is the friends

00:10:56.801 --> 00:10:57.801
and family test.

00:10:57.801 --> 00:11:01.470
I know that Tim is going to be talking about
this in detail.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:08.060
I do think this is a very, very significant
thing for the NHS and I think when people

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:14.670
look back in the future, this will be seen
not only as a significant point when the NHS

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:21.760
started to demonstrate that it was listening
directly to patients, but also a really important

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.930
mechanism by which people can measure whether
they really are living up to the idea that

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:33.160
patients and the public should be treated
like customers in all of that.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:38.740
I think there are indications already that
many organisations in the NHS are absorbing

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.130
this information and are making real changes
to improve things for patients.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:46.250
Berwick Report
Finally I would like to mention the Berwick

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:47.570
Report which has come out.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:55.810
We will be bringing a more formal report to
the Board about what our actions are in relation

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:59.670
to patient safety, a critical part of the
response to the Francis Inquiry.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.480
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: David, thank you very much

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:02.480
for that.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.700
Let me invite questions and comments from
Members of the Board.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:07.970
Ciarán?

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:15.630
Ciarán Devane: How did it land?

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.300
Are we taking it seriously enough?

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.980
My view, at least, is that there are a lot
of other organisations, not least Trusts,

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:27.780
who need to take it very seriously, as well;
so, are we confident that the link between

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.760
a good report and action on the ground is
there?

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.620
David Nicholson: Yes.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:37.711
It is very interesting, the Berwick report,
because it did not have hundreds and hundreds

00:12:37.711 --> 00:12:45.280
of recommendations in it, but it did talk
about a culture, an approach and a direction,

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:51.820
which I think in lots of ways will make a
more significant and more long term impact

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:56.510
on the NHS than, perhaps, some other things
which have been done.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:13:00.880
We are working really quite hard and one of
our domains, of course, is patient safety:

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:06.130
we have responsibilities that were given to
us around the old national patient safety

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:12.820
organisation; we have a Director of Patient
Safety, as well.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:16.650
We are, therefore, working very hard to see
how we can do that and part of that, I think,

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:22.000
is, 'How do we get it embedded in organisations
across the system as a whole?'

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.980
To be fair, there is a lot of enthusiasm for
making this happen.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:30.330
I think some of the things we have done around
academic health science centres and the way

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:36.340
we are bringing organisations together to
think about how they are taking work forward

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:44.390
will enable us to build that kind of collective
response to patient to safety.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:49.370
One of the things Don Berwick says is that
you need people to work together across organisations

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:52.600
and systems to enable it to happen and I think
we have an infrastructure there which would

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:57.490
really help us do that.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:06.320
We had a programme board for NHS IQ last week,
where we talked about how we could shift their

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:12.100
work and priorities to support patient safety
activities, particularly in terms of the improvement

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:17.800
science that you need to make things happen;
and I know that we are talking about how we

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:22.850
can build a coalition of leadership across
the NHS, not just us but other organisations,

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:24.180
to enable it to happen.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:29.180
Consequently, although it did not get as many
headlines as some of the other documents which

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:33.500
have come out, I think it will be a much longer
lasting report.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:36.260
In a sense, however, the Members of the Board
will make our own judgments about that when

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:43.850
we bring a firm set of proposals to the Board
about how we take it forward.

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:48.300
Malcolm Grant: That will build on the paper
that we are seeing today on the response to

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.720
the Francis Report and Winterbourne View.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:51.810
David Nicholson: Yes.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:52.900
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:53.900
Thank you very much.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:56.530
Is there anything else from Chief Executives?

00:14:56.530 --> 00:15:04.800
Margaret Casely Hayford: No responses can
have a dramatically disproportionate impact

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.020
on scores, which is obviously a very sound
point.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:12.450
Do you have plans for refreshing interest
periodically, to ensure an upwards trajectory?

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:13.450
David Nicholson: Yes.

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:14.890
I do not know whether Tim wants to respond.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:19.290
Tim Kelsey: I was going to cover that in the
next item but I am happy to deal with it now.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.560
Malcolm Grant: I think you should bring it
into the next item.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.760
If there is nothing more, then, for David,
may we move on to the next item, which is

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:25.760
the Patient and Public Voice?

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:26.760
Tim, again, building on what I was saying
earlier, the NHS belongs to the people and

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:27.390
the Patient and Public Voice is our guiding
principle.

